foreign  I'm a restorative dentist located in  Southern California and the owner of a  dental educational Channel called smile  influencers one tooth has a broken front  tooth another tooth has a cavity  underneath an old crown and the third  tooth has a broken filling and they all  need to be fixed  this case shows a broken tooth on a  mannequin  here I'm using a thin Diamond bur that I  use to create a 45 degree bevel onto the  tooth surface the white stuff you see  here is Teflon tape that I applied to  the adjacent teeth in order to protect  the adjacent teeth from the chemicals  that I'm about to use the blue stuff  that I'm using is phosphoric acid  etching which essentially prepares the  tooth for bonding I make sure to wash  off the etching thoroughly in order to  decrease the likelihood of  post-operative sensitivity the clear  stuff that I'm applying is a bonding  agent that I apply to the prepare to  surface and a little bit beyond I am  curing the tooth with an LED curing  light which essentially polymerizes any  light cured resin based Composites the  blue putty that you see here is a  hydrophilic VPS impression material it  is a guide that helps us in many cases  here you can see us using composite  resin packed in incremental layers to  replace that missing two structure and  hopefully create a filling that is very  strong  and a lot of these have different colors  that allow us to pick a shade that  closely matches our patients teeth  we also have different composite resins  that have different translucency  properties different opaque properties  and this allows us to create a filling  that looks as natural as possible in  this particular case since it's on a  typodon we're just using one shade to  give you an example of how to build up  that missing two structure  after I use a series of discs that go  from coarse to fine to polish The  Filling to make sure there's no Ledges  and make sure that it's as smooth as  possible  now when patients break their teeth it  can be due to a variety of causes trauma  let's say you get into a fight and  someone punches you falling down onto a  hard surface like concrete anytime you  put a strong force onto your front teeth  you can put it in jeopardy  foreign  this tooth has a crown with an axis hole  in the middle of it and there is a large  cavity that's developing underneath that  hole crowns are very strong and they're  typically bonded onto the tooth and one  of the safest ways to remove a crown is  by using a little bit of Leverage to  take the crown off I use a variety of  large Diamond Burrs in order to clean  the majority of the tooth cavity and at  the very end I use a round carbide  Brewer that runs at a slower speed in  order to get the very last bits of the  cavity  foreign  agent is then used we use a micro brush  to apply the bonding agent onto the  tooth core paste is a composite resin  system that has a high compressive  strength and it replaces a lot of the  lost to structure  and we use an LED curing light to cure  the outermost layer of the core paste  although it will also naturally Harden  over time  here we are fabricating an acrylic  temporary crown we use temporary cement  called temp bond to place our temporary  crown onto the tooth this essentially  maintains the space until our lab  technician finishes fabricating a new  dental crown  typically cavities form from poor oral  hygiene maybe not brushing or flossing  the right way or having a diet that's  high in sugar  this tooth has a broken filling with a  large cavity developing between the  filling and their natural tooth in order  to clean the Decay out I first have to  remove the existing filling and in this  particular case I'm using a diamond Burr  to remove all of the old composite resin  after that's removed I use a round  carbide Burr that's used on a slower  speed to make sure that I remove any  infected Dentin or areas where there is  a cavity present until we have nice  healthy tooth structure  in this particular case I'm using a  bonding agent called clear fill SE Bond  the primer in bond essentially prepare  the tooth surface for the application of  our composite resin filling material in  order for the filling material to  successfully adhere this is called  flowable resin it's less viscous  compared to other resins but it does a  great job at sealing margins and  preventing post-operative sensitivity  packable composite resins are highly  viscous resins that contain a high  volume of filler particles which gives  them a stiff consistency  at the very end we are adjusting the  shape of the filling in order to make  the occlusion of the patient feel as  natural as possible we're hoping that  when our patient moves left and right  front and back that there is no  interference between the patient's  motions and the new filling that's in  place  I always tell my patients that there are  things that you can do to protect your  teeth like brushing and flossing in the  morning and at night avoid dangerous  habits like opening packages with your  teeth because although our teeth are  very strong they can break and we want  to be very careful and conscientious  about what we put in our mouth

